h L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 13 Oct 2025 (this version), latest version 15 Oct 2025 (cite5†v2 )]\nL9: # Title:Latent Refinement Decoding: Enhancing Diffusion-Based Language Models by Refining Belief States\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Qinglin Zhu , cite7†Yizhen Yao , cite8†Runcong Zhao , cite9†Yanzheng Xiang , cite10†Amrutha Saseendran , cite11†Chen Jin , cite12†Philip Alexander Teare , cite13†Bin Liang , cite14†Yulan He , cite15†Lin Gui L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Latent Refinement Decoding: Enhancing Diffusion-Based Language Models by Refining Belief States, by Qinglin Zhu and 9 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite16†View PDF cite17†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Autoregressive (AR) models remain the standard for natural language generation but still suffer from high latency due to strictly sequential decoding. Recent diffusion-inspired approaches, such as LlaDA and Dream, mitigate this by generating in parallel, yet they suffer from two core limitations: information loss, as predictive distributions for non-finalized tokens are discarded at each step, and premature commitment, where local decisions are made without sufficient global coordination.\nL17: We introduce Latent Refinement Decoding (LRD), a two-stage framework with Latent Refinement and a Predictive Feedback Loop. The first stage maintains masked positions as distributional mixtures of predicted tokens and the mask embedding, allowing the model to establish more globally consistent beliefs. The second stage progressively finalizes confident tokens while retaining uncertain ones for iterative feedback.\nL18: KL-divergence dynamics provide a principled and reliable criterion for convergence and early stopping. Experiments across coding (HumanEval +6.3, MBPP +2.6) and reasoning (GSM8K +2.9, MATH500 +3.8) show that LRD improves accuracy while delivering speedups of up to 10.6x, making it a strong and versatile alternative for parallel sequence generation.\nL19: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite18†arXiv:2510.11052 [cs.CL]\nL21:    | (or cite19†arXiv:2510.11052v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL22:    | cite20†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.11052†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Qinglin Zhu [cite21†view email ]\nL26: [v1] Mon, 13 Oct 2025 06:38:13 UTC (1,721 KB)\nL27: cite5†[v2] Wed, 15 Oct 2025 10:33:35 UTC (1,721 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled Latent Refinement Decoding: Enhancing Diffusion-Based Language Models by Refining Belief States, by Qinglin Zhu and 9 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * cite16†View PDF L36:   * cite17†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite22†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite23†view license†creativecommons.org L40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: cs.CL\nL43: \nL44: cite24†< prev |   cite25†next > L45: \nL46: cite26†new | cite27†recent | cite28†2025-10 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite29†cs L51: \nL52: ### References & Citations\nL53: \nL54:   * cite30†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L55:   * cite31†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L56:   * cite32†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L57: \nL58: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL59: \nL60: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL61: \nL62: [Button: ×]\nL63: \nL64: loading...\nL65: \nL66: Data provided by:\nL67: \nL68: ### Bookmark\nL69: \nL70: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL71: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL72: \nL73: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL74: \nL75: Bibliographic Explorer (cite33†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL76: \nL77: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL78: \nL79: Connected Papers (cite34†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL80: \nL81: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL82: \nL83: Litmaps (cite35†What is Litm